Gate: cargo test -p phosphor-machines. Verify DIP behavior is unchanged by round-tripping each migrated machines bank values through dip_bank_value/set_dip_bank_value.

frontend/src/movie.rs — a tee wrapper over InputConfigurable, NOT a subscriber on
binding resolution.

    struct Recording<'a, M: ?Sized> { inner: &'a mut M, sink: &'a mut MovieRecorder }

    impl<M: InputConfigurable + ?Sized> InputConfigurable for Recording<'_, M> {
        fn input_controls(&self) -> &'static [InputControl] { self.inner.input_controls() }
        fn handle_input(&mut self, e: InputEvent) {
            self.sink.push_event(e);
            self.inner.handle_input(e);
        }
        fn release_all_inputs(&mut self) {
            self.sink.push_release_all();
            self.inner.release_all_inputs();
        }
    }

When armed, the frontend passes &mut Recording{..} where it passes `machine`
today. input::dispatch (frontend/src/input.rs:569) and input::resync (:711) are
NOT modified — both are already generic over M: InputConfigurable + ?Sized. No
future input path can bypass the recorder without also bypassing the trait.

WHY NOT a BindingSet subscriber: it misses resync, which emits real InputEvents
after a reset or state load with no corresponding SDL event, and it would not
cover release_all_inputs at all.

release_all_inputs is recorded as ITS OWN record, never decomposed into
per-control button releases: ccastles, marble and missile_command override it to
clear trackball accumulators the default loop does not touch.

UX: F2 = reset + arm recording, not "capture from here". A movie must start from
a known state so the artifact depends only on the ROM set. Write-on-stop,
atomically (*.tmp then rename). Also emit Dip records when set_dip_option is
called mid-session.

PREREQUISITE TO CHECK FIRST: playing through a self-test on every take is ~3000
frames / ~50 s on galaga. The unthrottled mode the clock loop already knows about
(frontend/src/emulator.rs:966) covers it IF it is reachable from a binding.
Confirm a fast-forward action exists; if not, add one here — without it the
capture workflow is unpleasant enough that nobody will use it.

Also add the rewind mutual-exclusion guard if the rewind epic has already landed
(rewind disabled while a recording is armed).

Rejected for v1: prepending a save_state blob so recording can start mid-session.
It re-couples the movie to SAVE_VERSION, inflates a ~1 KB artifact to tens of KB,
and every save-format change would invalidate the committed corpus.

Found while doing phosphor-emulator-vy6l (giving every don't-care cycle an
address). These two are cycle-COUNT gaps, not addressing gaps, so they were out
of that issue's scope — nothing silent to fix, just cycles we never spend.

CWAI (0x3C). Ours is: read operand, then 12 pushes, then wait. The hardware
spends two more cycles between the operand read and the pushes — one re-driving
PC, one at $FFFF:

    @m_cc &= read_opcode_arg();
    @dummy_read_opcode_arg(0);
    @dummy_vma(1);
    ... PUSH_REGISTERS ...

INTERRUPT_VECTOR. Ours reads the two vector bytes and nothing else
(`execute_interrupt` cycles 20/21, the path CWAI takes on wake). The hardware
brackets the vector fetch with a /VMA cycle on each side:

    @dummy_vma(1);
    @m_pc.b.h = read_vector(0);
    @m_pc.b.l = read_vector(1);
    @dummy_vma(1);

The full NMI/IRQ/FIRQ/SWI entry sequences DO have those brackets (they are
cycles 14 and 17 of `interrupt_full`, now driving $FFFF) — it is only the
vector-only path used to resume from CWAI that is missing them.

Both are excluded from cross-validation (SYNC and CWAI cannot single-step with
no interrupt source), so nothing catches this today. Fixing it changes CWAI's
cycle count, which is why it wants its own change with its own timing check
rather than riding along.
